A browser port of [enp-toolkit-secure](https://github.com/CSSluckyyy095/enp-toolkit-secure),
rebuilt as a Manifest V3 Chrome extension. All encryption happens **locally in your
browser** — nothing is ever sent to a server.

## What's the same as the desktop app

- All 7 methods: AES-128 / AES-192 / AES-256 (CBC + PBKDF2-SHA256, 200k iterations),
  ChaCha20, TripleDES, Fernet, RSA-Hybrid (RSA-2048-OAEP + AES-256)
- Text mode and File mode
- Password strength meter, dark GitHub-style theme, same layout and shortcuts
  (Ctrl+E encrypt, Ctrl+D decrypt, Ctrl+S swap, Ctrl+L clear)
- Fernet / RSA key vault, protected by a master password (PBKDF2, 150k iterations)

## What had to change (browser ≠ desktop)

A Chrome extension cannot run Python/Tkinter or touch your filesystem by path, so:

| Desktop app | Extension |
|---|---|
| `fernet.key.enc`, `rsa_private.pem.enc` on disk | Stored in `chrome.storage.local` (still encrypted with your master password, same PBKDF2+AES wrap) |
| File dialog picks any path | `<input type="file">` — you select a file, browser reads its bytes |
| Saves output next to the input file | Downloads the encrypted/decrypted file via the browser's download flow |
| Tkinter window | A full browser tab (click the toolbar icon to open it) |

The actual cryptography is native **Web Crypto API** for AES, PBKDF2, and RSA-OAEP,
plus a hand-written ChaCha20 (verified byte-for-byte against the RFC 8439 test
vectors) and `crypto-js` for TripleDES, since browsers don't expose either natively.

## Install locally (works right now, no store needed)

1. Download/unzip this folder.
2. Open `chrome://extensions` in Chrome.
3. Turn on **Developer mode** (top-right toggle).
4. Click **Load unpacked** → select the `extension` folder.
5. Click the puzzle-piece icon in the toolbar → pin **ENP Tool Kit**.
6. Click it any time to open the toolkit in a new tab.

## Publishing it publicly (so anyone can add it from the Chrome Web Store)

This part needs to happen from **your own** Google account — I can't do it for you,
since it needs your identity and a payment method:

1. Go to the [Chrome Web Store Developer Dashboard](https://chrome.google.com/webstore/devconsole).
2. Pay the one-time **$5 USD** registration fee (first time only).
3. Click **New Item**, upload `enp-toolkit-extension.zip` (already built in this folder).
4. Fill in the store listing: description, at least one 1280×800 screenshot of the
   app tab, and a privacy statement (easy here — you can honestly say **no data
   leaves the browser, nothing is collected**).
5. Submit for review. Google's review for a simple, no-permissions-abuse extension
   like this usually takes a few hours to a few days.
6. Once approved, it's public — share the Chrome Web Store link, and it'll show
   up in Chrome Web Store search too.

## Security notes worth knowing before you publish it

- This is a faithful, from-scratch reimplementation for portfolio/demo purposes —
  it hasn't been through professional security review or a paid audit. Fine to
  showcase; call it "for educational/demo use" in the listing, same as the
  original repo already does.
- `crypto-js` is vendored locally (no CDN, no remote code) so it passes Chrome
  Web Store's Manifest V3 remote-code policy.
- Because `chrome.storage.local` isn't a hardware-backed vault, treat the master
  password vault the same way you'd treat the original app's `.enc` files —
  don't rely on it for anything you can't afford to lose if your profile is
  compromised.
